{"kind":"report","text":"Custody lost-update repair is complete and merged on main; this closes my promise. The work landed through the successor requests codex filed when artifact-succession defects retired the original lineage: request #10431 sealed by the merge of 62e45b8f9 (recut of tested heads 6b415ccf and e2326e24 onto current main), then request #10639 sealed by the merge of 23f691d66 repairing the five ratified review findings. Conditions of the original request, as met on current main: (1) Chosen mechanism: reload-and-merge — UpdateConfig holds an exclusive advisory lock on .config.lock across one whole load-modify-store and reloads from disk inside it. Conflict rule stated explicitly, not last-writer-wins renamed: the caller declares exactly which fields it changes and only those declared fields are merged onto the freshly reloaded content; everything else keeps the on-disk value. Patch-only-the-changed-field was rejected: every future Config writer would need per-field plumbing and one forgotten call site silently reintroduces the defect. Per-actor custody files were rejected: they fragment one document's atomicity across many files and change the custody contract for every reader. Platforms without advisory locking refuse updates loudly rather than silently losing them. (2) All whole-Config writers covered: internal/app and host both go through the locked update path; creating a first configuration stays available without the lock. (3) The misleading SaveConfig doc comment is corrected and the four custody paths are distinguished on docs/reference/architecture.md. (4) Regression written from desired behaviour in internal/apphost/config_update_test.go — a second process adds an actor, the first persists an unrelated field, the new actor must survive — mutation-checked during the reviewed chain. (5) The durable log was never modified. Worktrees were removed after merge and main is pushed to origin. Re-verified today at origin/main: go test ./internal/apphost/ passes.","body":{"branch":"request/custody-lost-update","head":"23f691d6637616857466611dd1637f584434f195"}}